WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:09.260
When President Trump meets South Korea's
new president Moon Jae-In at their first

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.940
Washington summit in June 2017, the two
leaders will have a chance to forge a

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:17.990
personal bond that could strengthen the
US-ROK alliance against growing North

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:23.150
Korean threats. But the meeting also
entails non-trivial risks. President Moon

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:27.410
favors a departure from the current
joint US-ROK approach towards North

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:32.930
Korea and return instead to a sunshine
policy, the more conciliatory engagement

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:36.530
oriented posture with which Seoul
experimented during the Clinton and

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:40.850
George W. Bush administrations. President
Moon wishes to reverse, or at least

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:45.409
suspend, South Korea's implementation of
THAAD missile defense. He wishes to reopen

00:00:45.409 --> 00:00:49.640
the ROK sponsored Kaesong Industrial
Complex in the north, shut down by his

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.299
predecessor on suspicion it was funneling
money to Kim Jong-Un's nuclear and

00:00:53.299 --> 00:00:56.930
missile program. Also, he seems
uncomfortable pressing North Korea on

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:01.280
its human rights violations. Last time
around sunshine policies caused US-ROK

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.360
relations to suffer and North Korea
profited. Will moonshine fare better?

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:06.340
Stay tuned.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.860
To learn more about my take on
North Korea, check the links in the

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:15.140
description below. Also, let us know what
other topics you want a AEI scholars to

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.190
cover in 60 seconds.

